const cs = db.collection('orders').watch();
for await (const change of cs) {
console.log(change);
}
Each change document looks like:
{
"_id": { "_data": "82..." }, // resume token
"operationType": "insert", // insert | update | replace | delete | drop | rename | invalidate
"clusterTime": Timestamp(...),
"wallTime": ISODate(...),
"ns": { "db": "shop", "coll": "orders" },
"documentKey": { "_id": "o5" },
"fullDocument": { "_id": "o5", "user_id": 1, ... } // present for inserts
}
For updates:
{
"operationType": "update",
"documentKey": { "_id": "o3" },
"updateDescription": {
"updatedFields": { "status": "shipped" },
"removedFields": [],
"truncatedArrays": []
}
}
For deletes, only documentKey is present (the full document is gone).

The first argument to watch() is a normal aggregation pipeline applied to events:
const cs = db.collection('orders').watch([
{ $match: {
operationType: { $in: ["insert", "update"] },
"fullDocument.status": "paid"
}},
{ $project: { _id: 1, documentKey: 1, fullDocument: 1, operationType: 1 } }
])
The server applies the filter before sending — only matching events reach you.
For update events the fullDocument is not present by default; see next chapter.

db.runCommand({
collMod: "orders",
changeStreamPreAndPostImages: { enabled: true }
})
db.collection('orders').watch([], {
fullDocument: "required",
fullDocumentBeforeChange:"required"
})
Now every event includes both:
fullDocument — the doc as-of after the change.
fullDocumentBeforeChange — the doc as-of before.
Useful for: diff-based audit, before/after replication to another store, "what exactly changed".
Cost: storing pre-images increases write overhead; enable per collection only when needed.

Every change event has _id = a resume token. If your consumer crashes, persist the last processed token, then resume:
const cs = db.collection('orders').watch([], {
resumeAfter: lastToken // or startAfter: lastToken
});
resumeAfter — fails if the token is no longer in the oplog.
startAfter — like resumeAfter but tolerant of invalidate events (e.g., collection drop).
startAtOperationTime — start at a cluster time instead of a token.
Pattern:
let lastToken = await loadLastToken();
const cs = db.collection('orders').watch([], {
resumeAfter: lastToken,
fullDocument: 'updateLookup'
});
for await (const change of cs) {
await processChange(change);
await saveLastToken(change._id);
}
If the consumer is down longer than the oplog window (Post 10), the token expires → must re-sync from scratch.

const cs = db.collection('products').watch([], { fullDocument: 'updateLookup' });
for await (const change of cs) {
const id = change.documentKey._id;
switch (change.operationType) {
case 'insert':
case 'update':
case 'replace':
await redis.set(`product:${id}`, JSON.stringify(change.fullDocument));
break;
case 'delete':
await redis.del(`product:${id}`);
break;
}
}
App reads from Redis; cache stays warm + accurate automatically. Better than a TTL-based cache when staleness matters.

const cs = db.collection('orders').watch([], {
fullDocument: 'required',
fullDocumentBeforeChange:'required'
});
for await (const change of cs) {
await db.collection('audit_orders').insertOne({
at: change.wallTime,
op: change.operationType,
document_id: change.documentKey._id,
before: change.fullDocumentBeforeChange,
after: change.fullDocument,
by: change.lsid // session id, if available
});
}
The audit collection becomes a tamper-evident change history (especially if combined with TTL for retention + S3 Object Lock for archived snapshots).

Pre-compute daily_revenue with $merge (Post 06), then keep it fresh via change streams:
const cs = db.collection('orders').watch([
{ $match: { 'fullDocument.status': 'paid' } }
], { fullDocument: 'updateLookup' });
for await (const change of cs) {
if (!change.fullDocument) continue;
const day = trunc(change.fullDocument.at, 'day');
await db.collection('daily_revenue').updateOne(
{ _id: day },
{ $inc: { revenue: change.fullDocument.total, orders: 1 } },
{ upsert: true }
);
}
Reactive instead of batch — daily_revenue updates within seconds of the order.
For exactly-once semantics, watch out: if your consumer crashes after applying but before persisting the resume token, you'll double-count on restart. Mitigations:
Idempotent updates (e.g., use the order _id to dedupe).
Transactional consumer (persist token + apply update in one transaction).
Outbox table on the source side (the order is the source of truth; the view is rebuildable).

Stream MongoDB changes into Elasticsearch / OpenSearch / Algolia for full-text search; into Redis for caching; into BigQuery / Snowflake for analytics; into another MongoDB cluster for cross-region replication.
const cs = db.collection('articles').watch([], { fullDocument: 'updateLookup' });
for await (const change of cs) {
switch (change.operationType) {
case 'insert':
case 'update':
case 'replace':
await openSearch.index({
index: 'articles',
id: change.documentKey._id,
body: change.fullDocument
});
break;
case 'delete':
await openSearch.delete({ index: 'articles', id: change.documentKey._id });
break;
}
}
Atlas does this natively via Atlas Triggers (Post 16) — a serverless function fires on every change event. No infra to run.

For serverless use, Atlas runs your code on every change event:
// Atlas Trigger function — runs on each change
exports = async function(changeEvent) {
const fullDoc = changeEvent.fullDocument;
// ... your logic
};
No infrastructure; pay per invocation. Post 16 covers Triggers + Atlas Functions deeply.
For local / self-hosted, the long-running consumer pattern (Chapter 5) is the equivalent.
